import{_ as a,I as i,g as t,j as o}from"./chunks/framework.CjoWWfVe.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sales (Receipts)","description":"Registering sales via the POS terminal, managing sales channels, shipping logistics, and calculating margin efficiency (Time ROI).","frontmatter":{"title":"Sales (Receipts)","description":"Registering sales via the POS terminal, managing sales channels, shipping logistics, and calculating margin efficiency (Time ROI).","order":9},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/production/sales.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/production/sales.md"}'),s={name:"docs/spaces/production/sales.md"};function n(r,e,l,c,p,h){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">📌 Documentation Under Development</p><p><strong>Dear Users!</strong></p><p>Since OmniCraft ERP is currently in an active beta stage, with regular updates and expansions, the reference materials are also being updated gradually.</p><p>At this time, please treat the documentation as a guide—it is currently incomplete, may temporarily lack descriptions for certain features, or contain minor inaccuracies.</p></div><h1 id="🧾-sales-the-pos-terminal-efficiency-analysis" tabindex="-1">🧾 Sales: The POS Terminal &amp; Efficiency Analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#🧾-sales-the-pos-terminal-efficiency-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🧾 Sales: The POS Terminal &amp; Efficiency Analysis&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <strong>&quot;Sales&quot;</strong> tab is split into three functional areas: a form for registering a new sale (the POS terminal), an interactive log of processed receipts, and an analytical table measuring the return on your working time (Time ROI).</p><p>Processing a sale in the program is a key action that instantly deducts finished products from showcase stock, logs cash in your Cash Flow Ledger, and calculates net profit.</p><hr><h2 id="🖥️-processing-a-sale-pos-terminal" tabindex="-1">🖥️ Processing a Sale (POS Terminal) <a class="header-anchor" href="#🖥️-processing-a-sale-pos-terminal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🖥️ Processing a Sale (POS Terminal)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The form at the top of the screen allows you to record a sale quickly. To do this, fill out the following fields:</p><ol><li><strong>Product Selection:</strong> The dropdown displays only finished products currently available in showroom stock (quantity &gt; 0).</li><li><strong>Sales Channel:</strong> The channel where the transaction occurred (e.g., <em>Etsy, personal website, social media, or a marketplace</em>). Your final retail price will adapt according to the selected channel.</li><li><strong>Quantity:</strong> The number of items purchased by the client.</li><li><strong>Shipping (Logistics):</strong> A precise economic tool that the program calculates based on strict rules.</li></ol><h3 id="🚚-economic-logic-of-shipping" tabindex="-1">🚚 Economic Logic of Shipping <a class="header-anchor" href="#🚚-economic-logic-of-shipping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🚚 Economic Logic of Shipping&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The program offers two shipping payment modes that impact your financials differently:</p><ul><li><strong>&quot;Paid by Buyer&quot; (Client) Mode:</strong> The client transferred funds for both the product and separately compensated you for the shipping cost. <ul><li><em>In Cash Flow:</em> A full inflow is logged (Product Price + Shipping Cost).</li><li><em>Outflow:</em> A corresponding shipping expense is logged.</li><li><em>Impact on Profit:</em> Neutral (the shipping cost received directly matches the shipping expense). However, your Cash Flow Ledger reflects a completely accurate cash turnover.</li></ul></li><li><strong>&quot;Paid by Workshop&quot; (Expense) Mode:</strong> You provided the client with free shipping (or covered it at your own expense). <ul><li><em>In Cash Flow:</em> A net inflow is logged for the product price, and an outflow is logged for the logistics company&#39;s shipping fee.</li><li><em>Impact on Profit:</em> Negative. The shipping cost is deducted directly from your net profit on this transaction.</li></ul></li></ul><hr><h2 id="📈-managing-sales-channels" tabindex="-1">📈 Managing Sales Channels <a class="header-anchor" href="#📈-managing-sales-channels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;📈 Managing Sales Channels&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Different platforms charge varying commissions and require different pricing strategies. In the program, you can configure individual markups for each channel.</p><p>Click the gear icon ⚙️ next to the channel selector to open the management window:</p><ul><li>You can add a new channel (e.g., <em>“Shopify”</em> or <em>“Amazon”</em>).</li><li>Specify a <strong>custom markup in %</strong> (it will be added to the basic recommended retail price calculated in the Calculator).</li></ul><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Fixed Prices for Specific Channels</p><p>If a percentage-based markup is not ideal and you want to establish a strict, flat price for a specific platform (for example, you want an item to sell for exactly <code>$45</code> on Shopify and <code>$40</code> at local markets), you can do so in the <a href="./showcase">Finished Goods</a> tab by setting fixed prices in the product profile. In this case, the calculator will yield to your manual pricing rules.</p></div><hr><h2 id="🧾-receipt-history-sales-log" tabindex="-1">🧾 Receipt History (Sales Log) <a class="header-anchor" href="#🧾-receipt-history-sales-log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🧾 Receipt History (Sales Log)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bottom-left portion of the screen displays the history of all processed sales.</p><ul><li>The table displays the transaction date, sales channel, product name, quantity, and the <strong>net profit</strong> earned on each receipt.</li><li>The table supports pagination (paged viewing of 15 records), ensuring a smooth, lag-free interface even with thousands of entries in the database.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="🏆-product-rating-time-roi-—-return-on-time" tabindex="-1">🏆 Product Rating (Time ROI — Return on Time) <a class="header-anchor" href="#🏆-product-rating-time-roi-—-return-on-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;🏆 Product Rating (Time ROI — Return on Time)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bottom-right portion of the screen holds one of the most valuable analytical tables for a business owner—the <strong>Product Rating by Return on Time</strong>.</p><p>Traditional accounting only displays absolute profit (for example, selling a bag nets you <code>$50</code> in profit, while a passport cover nets you <code>$10</code>). This creates the illusion that crafting bags is always more profitable.</p><p><strong>OmniCraft ERP calculates Time ROI (hourly profit):</strong> The program takes the net profit from a product&#39;s sale and divides it by the number of hours you physically spent crafting it (based on the time norm specified in the Calculator).</p><p>$$\\text{Hourly Profit (Time ROI)} = \\frac{\\text{Net Profit from Product Sales}}{\\text{Total Hours Spent Crafting Completed Items}}$$</p><h3 id="why-is-this-necessary" tabindex="-1">Why is this necessary? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-is-this-necessary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why is this necessary?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This table clearly reveals the &quot;hidden killers&quot; of your time and your true profit engines:</p><ul><li>You might discover that a complex bag yields only <strong>$3 per hour</strong> due to massive labor hours.</li><li>Meanwhile, a simple passport cover yields <strong>$15 per hour</strong> because it is crafted in 20 minutes.</li><li><em>Business Takeaway:</em> To maximize your earnings, it is economically smarter to focus on volume sales of passport covers, while the price of the bag should be raised immediately in the Calculator.</li></ul>',30)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
